62. Unique Paths

🧠 Problem
Given an m x n grid:

Start at (0,0)
Reach (m-1, n-1)
Allowed moves:
Right →
Down ↓
👉 Find total number of unique paths

🔑 Core Idea (MOST IMPORTANT)

👉 Think backward

At cell (i, j), ask:

“Where did I come from?”

From top → (i-1, j)
From left → (i, j-1)
🔁 Recurrence

dp[i][j]=dp[i−1][j]+dp[i][j−1]

👉 Total ways = ways from top + ways from left

🧠 State Definition
dp[i][j] = number of ways to reach cell (i, j)
🧩 Base Case
dp[0][0] = 1

👉 Only one way: start position

⚠️ Boundary Handling
if i > 0 → can come from top
if j > 0 → can come from left

👉 Prevents out-of-bounds access

🧠 Intuition (Backward Thinking)

To reach (i, j):

You must have been at (i-1, j) OR (i, j-1)

👉 So:ways to (i,j) = ways to (top) + ways to (left)

code:
class Solution {
    public int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {
        int[][] dp = new int[m][n];

        for(int i = 0; i < m; i++){
            for(int j = 0; j < n; j++){

                if(i == 0 && j == 0){
                    dp[i][j] = 1;
                    continue;
                }

                int up = 0;
                int left = 0;

                if(i > 0) up = dp[i-1][j];
                if(j > 0) left = dp[i][j-1];

                dp[i][j] = up + left;
            }
        }

        return dp[m-1][n-1];
    }
}
